course: dictionary
lesson: 14670
title: Premises: buildings or ideas
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Premises is one of them. Look at the words around it to find out which meaning the writer means.
explain: Most of the time, premises means the buildings and land that a business, school or organisation uses. «No smoking on these premises» means no smoking anywhere in the buildings or the grounds, not just in one room.
explain: Premises has another meaning in arguments. Premises are the statements that an argument is built on. «All dogs need walks. Max is a dog. So Max needs a walk.» The first two statements are the premises, and the last one is the conclusion.
explain: Check the clues. If the sentence is about a school, a shop or a sign, think buildings. If it is about an argument or whether something must be true, think ideas.
fact: Premises (plural) means the buildings and land that a business, school or organisation uses.
fact: A premise (singular) is one idea or statement that an argument is built on.
fact: «On the premises» means inside the buildings or grounds of a place.
fact: «Leave the premises» means go out of the buildings or grounds.
fact: In logic, if the premises are true, the conclusion should be true.
exercise: «Dogs are not allowed on the premises.» What does premises mean here? | The weather outside | * The buildings and land of the place | The front door only | The ideas in an argument
exercise: «All birds have feathers. A robin is a bird. So a robin has feathers.» Which lines are the premises? | * The first two statements | Only the last sentence, which is the conclusion | Only the word robin | The word feathers
exercise: «Please leave the premises by the back gate.» What does leave the premises mean? | Count all the rooms | Paint the walls a new colour | Stay inside and go to sleep | * Go out of the buildings or grounds
test: «The school premises include the playground, the field and the hall.» What are the school premises? | The teachers' ideas about lessons | A list of school rules | * The buildings and grounds of the school | Only the playground
test: In an argument, what are the premises? | The final answer the argument reaches | * The statements the argument is built on | The name of the person arguing | Any word that rhymes with conclusion
exam: «Smoking is not allowed on these premises, so please go outside.» Which meaning of premises is used in this sentence? | The name of a famous lawyer | * The buildings and land of the business | A kind of weather | The statements an argument is built on
